---
<InsightArticle title={e.title} description={e.description} date="2026-11-14" type={e.type as 'Analysis' | 'Explainer'} readTime="5 min" standfirst="In our invented audit, 95 of 100 citation pointers resolve, but only eighty passages support their claims. Check the passage against the whole sentence before counting the answer as grounded.">

<p>A clickable citation is a useful start. It lets a reviewer inspect the material behind an answer. The next question is whether that material establishes what the answer says. A real passage about a company does not support every statement the model might make about that company.</p>
<p>The <a href={e.sources[0].url}>Claude citations guide</a>, read on 14 November 2026, describes source locations and returned cited text. Those fields help locate evidence. Our proposed audit checks location validity and claim support separately rather than treating the presence of a citation as acceptance.</p>
<h2>Make the unit a claim</h2>
<p>Split an answer into statements that can be checked. Preserve the original sentence beside any split so that qualifications are not lost. A sentence saying a feature is available in a particular region on a particular plan needs evidence for those conditions, not merely for the feature's existence.</p>
<p>For the teaching example, assume 100 claims with one citation each. Ninety-five pointers resolve to the intended source. Eighty of those passages support the complete claim under the review rule.</p>
<WorkedTable headers={["Audit gate in a synthetic set","Claim-citation pairs","Share of all pairs"]} rows={examples['citations-pointer-and-proof']} caption="Constructed counts for 100 pairs, one cited passage per claim. Supported claims are a subset of valid pointers." />
<p>Pointer validity is 95%. Support across the original set is 80%. Among valid pointers, support is about 84.2%. Fifteen claims point somewhere real without being supported. Keeping the original denominator stops a citation-format success from hiding an evidence failure.</p>
<h2>Read the conditions around the passage</h2>
<p>Check the subject, date, unit and scope. A number about one product tier should not become a number about the entire service. A planned change should not become a shipped feature. Save the relevant qualification alongside the passage when reviewing the claim.</p>
<p>Also distinguish a source reporting its own result from an independent finding. A citation can accurately establish that a lab made a claim while leaving the claimed performance unverified. Write the answer to match that narrower evidence.</p>
<p>When several sources are required, define completeness before scoring. A comparison may need one passage for each side. Counting a claim as supported because one of its citations resolves would skip the comparison's second half.</p>
<h2>Keep the source version</h2>
<p>A pointer needs the document version it refers to. A character offset in revised text may identify a different sentence, while a page reference can change after a new cover sheet is added. Retain a stable source identifier and version or retrieval date suitable for your system.</p>
<p>Use the API's documented indexing conventions when implementing the viewer. The cited material should be shown exactly as returned or recovered from the matching source. Do not silently repair an invalid location by selecting a plausible passage and then count the original pointer as valid.</p>
<p>For public reports, publish only material you are allowed to quote and retain. A source link, brief permitted excerpt and review outcome can be enough. Private documents and account-specific records should not become public fixtures for convenience.</p>
<h2>Keep the failures actionable</h2>
<p>Label a missing pointer, a wrong location, a passage that only partly supports the claim and an outdated source separately. They suggest different repairs. A model change may not fix a viewer that opens the wrong document version.</p>
<p>Include questions whose answer is absent from the supplied documents. The correct behaviour may be to state that the material does not establish the answer. An unsupported answer with a decorative citation should fail the same review rule as an answer with no citation.</p>
<p>Record claim count, pointer-valid count, fully supported count and the review rule in the tracker. A citation becomes valuable evidence when somebody can follow it and verify the sentence it is attached to.</p>
<PractitionerNote evidence={e} />
</InsightArticle>
